UMOUNT(1)                    System Administration                   UMOUNT(1)

NAME
       umount - unmount file systems

SYNOPSIS
       umount [-flnrv] directory|source...
       umount -a [-flnrv] [-t types]

DESCRIPTION
       umount detaches mounted file systems: what mount(1) attached.  Each ar-
       gument is a mount point, or the source a file system is mounted from --
       a  device  (/dev/sdb1)  or  a tag naming one (UUID=, LABEL=, PARTUUID=,
       PARTLABEL=).  When several file systems are  mounted  on  top  of  each
       other at one directory, the topmost is unmounted.

       Before  a  file  system  is  detached, what is still buffered for it is
       written; a file system on which  files  are  open,  or  which  is  some
       process's working directory, is busy and stays mounted (see -l and -r).

       Unmounting needs root.

OPTIONS
       -a, --all
              Unmount every file system that can be, the most recently mounted
              first:  everything  but  the  root  and the kernel's own (/proc,
              /sys, /dev).

       -t, --types list
              With -a: only the file systems of these types (separated by com-
              mas; a no prefix excludes a type).

       -f, --force
              Force the unmount.  Accepted for compatibility:  files  open  on
              the file system still keep it busy.

       -l, --lazy
              Detach  the  file  system now, and finish unmounting it when the
              last file open on it is closed.

       -r, --read-only
              When the file system is busy, mount it read-only instead.

       -n, --no-mtab
              Accepted; there is no /etc/mtab to update.

       -v, --verbose
              Say what is unmounted.

       -h, --help
              Show a summary of the options.

       -V, --version
              Show the version.

EXIT STATUS
       0      Success.

       1      Wrong usage, or no permission.

       2      A system error.

       32     The unmount failed.

       64     Some of several unmounts failed.

FILES
       /proc/mounts
              What is mounted now.

EXAMPLES
       umount /mnt
       umount LABEL=STICK
       umount -l /media/stick

SEE ALSO
       mount(1), fstab(5)

